Rationale: Analyzer shows optimizer is favoring nominal peaks near the failure boundary: robust_performance_mc mean = 0.3545 (std 0.0543) while cliff_margin weight is only 0.5 and some designs remain nominally infeasible (worst cliff_margin = -0.0391). The robust estimator is noisy (16-sample MC). Increasing robustness/safety emphasis and adding a duplicate robust_performance_mc objective (independent MC draws) will (a) shift selection away from risky nominal peaks and (b) reduce selection variance by giving the optimizer two independent MC signals per candidate. Evidence: current robust_performance_mc weight = 1.0, cliff_margin weight = 0.5, robust mean = 0.3545, robust std = 0.0543, worst cliff_margin = -0.0391.

<decision>
{
  "failure_location": "objective",
  "root_cause_hypothesis": "Current objective weighting under-emphasizes robustness/safety (robust_performance_mc weight=1.0, cliff_margin weight=0.5), so the optimizer pursues high nominal values near the cliff; noisy 16-sample MC further destabilizes selection.",
  "intervention_target": "objective",
  "rationale": "Raise the optimizer's emphasis on expected robust performance and safety and add a duplicate MC-based robust objective to reduce selection variance. Analyzer numbers: robust_performance_mc mean=0.3545 (std=0.0543); cliff_margin weight is only 0.5 and worst cliff_margin = -0.0391, indicating some nominal infeasibility and risk of wasting budget near the cliff.",
  "proposed_change": {
    "ops": [
      {"op": "set_weight", "name": "robust_performance_mc", "weight": 2.0},
      {"op": "set_weight", "name": "cliff_margin", "weight": 1.0},
      {"op": "add_from_registry", "name": "robust_performance_mc", "optimization_direction": "maximize", "weight": 1.0}
    ]
  },
  "expected_benefit": "Stronger pressure toward candidates with higher expected performance under tolerance and safer margins (fewer nominal failures). The duplicate robust_performance_mc provides an implicit variance reduction (two independent MC estimates) so selection is less driven by single-sample noise.",
  "validation_plan": "Validator should observe, in the next accepted population, improved robust performance metrics (increase in population mean robust_performance_mc) and a reduction in nominal failures (fewer candidates with cliff_margin < 0). Also check that robust_performance_mc variability across candidates becomes more consistently informative (less selection driven by MC noise).",
  "confidence": 0.7
}
</decision>

The analyzer identifies noisy ranking from the 16-sample robust_performance_mc estimator as the primary shortfall; duplicating that objective will effectively average independent MC estimates and reduce selection noise without changing scorer code. I propose adding a second robust_performance_mc objective.

<decision>{
  "failure_location": "objective",
  "root_cause_hypothesis": "The robust_performance_mc scorer is unbiased but high-variance (only 16 MC samples), causing noisy candidate rankings and slowing optimization progress.",
  "intervention_target": "objective",
  "rationale": "robust_performance_mc uses 16 fixed MC samples per candidate (noisy). Population robust_performance_mc mean = 0.4384 (up +0.0839) and std = 0.0588; best = 0.5056. With only 16 samples, estimator variance can cause misranking of candidates, especially since several designs have small cliff_margin (min = 0.011). Duplicating the robust_performance_mc objective will run an independent 16-sample estimate twice per candidate, effectively averaging to ~32 samples and reducing estimator variance.",
  "proposed_change": {
    "ops": [
      {
        "op": "add_from_registry",
        "name": "robust_performance_mc",
        "optimization_direction": "maximize",
        "weight": 2.0
      }
    ]
  },
  "expected_benefit": "Averaging two independent 16-sample estimates (approx. halving variance, reducing SD by ~1/sqrt(2)) should reduce stochastic misranking, produce more reliable selection/elites, and speed convergence toward higher true expected robust performance.",
  "validation_plan": "Validator should observe improved selection stability and net improvement in robust_performance_mc (best or population mean) in subsequent iterations versus the current accepted state; specifically look for higher validated robust_performance_mc and reduced variability in repeated evaluations of top candidates.",
  "confidence": 0.70
}</decision>

I agree the shortfall is at the objective level: robustness is the limiting factor (robust mean 0.4602 vs nominal mean 0.6204) and there is no deterministic safety signal encouraging distance from the failure boundary. A small, targeted objective change adding cliff_margin (deterministic, cheap, and directly tied to the failure boundary) and reducing nominal's weight should push the optimizer away from designs that sit close to x0+x1=1 and thereby reduce failure probability under the noisy MC evaluations (which are also noisy: robust estimates use only 16 MC samples each). This is a minimal, focused change (2 ops) intended to improve true expected robust performance while avoiding unnecessary changes to the noisy robust_performance_mc scorers.

